From: Xeon Phi Revival Project
Subject: [PATCH] python: use a K1OM-supported sequential fence

Knights Corner does not implement the x86 `mfence` instruction used by
CPython's x86 atomic fallback. GCC's `__sync_synchronize()` emits a supported
locked operation for K1OM and retains the required compiler and CPU barrier
semantics.

---
 Include/internal/pycore_atomic.h | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Include/internal/pycore_atomic.h b/Include/internal/pycore_atomic.h
index 8e6d9c4..9d71810 100644
--- a/Include/internal/pycore_atomic.h
+++ b/Include/internal/pycore_atomic.h
@@ -131,7 +131,7 @@ _Py_atomic_thread_fence(_Py_memory_order order)
 _Py_atomic_thread_fence(_Py_memory_order order)
 {
     if (order != _Py_memory_order_relaxed)
-        __asm__ volatile("mfence":::"memory");
+        __sync_synchronize();
 }
 
 /* Tell the race checker about this operation's effects. */
